=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies entirely on router0, not on router1's WAN uplink as the ticket
assumed. The physical WAN segment (wan_r0_r1, 10.10.0.x/8) is fully operational:
both eth2 interfaces are UP with correct addresses, traffic crosses it without
loss, and router1's RIP daemon is running and correctly advertising all four of
its networks. The router1 side is blameless.

On router0, the FRR configuration file (/etc/frr/frr.conf) has been deleted and
the RIP daemon (ripd) is not running. As a result, router0 participates in no
routing protocol at all. Its kernel table contains only its three directly
connected prefixes (192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.2.0/24, 10.0.0.0/8) and has no
entries for the Office (192.168.3.0/24) or Principle (192.168.4.0/24) networks.
When an Office or Principle host sends a packet destined for a CS or IT host,
router1 can forward it toward router0 (router1 still holds RIP-learned routes
to 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24, though these will age out shortly). The
packet arrives at router0 and even reaches the destination host. The reply
packet, however, is sent to router0 as the default gateway; router0 has no route
to 192.168.3.0/24 or 192.168.4.0/24 and drops it, causing the ping to time out.

Root cause: router0's /etc/frr/frr.conf was removed and ripd stopped, stripping
router0 of all RIP routing capability.

Fix: Restore /etc/frr/frr.conf on router0 with a proper RIP stanza (router rip;
network 192.168.1.0/24; network 192.168.2.0/24; network 10.0.0.0/8; version 2),
enable ripd in /etc/frr/daemons, and restart FRR (systemctl restart frr).
=== END ===
